g on it. The two of them have been waiting since November, comparing what I give them, and I did not know that until last night.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nI hear that the waiting and the comparing have been sitting between you and them, and it’s hard not to feel caught in the middle. If it helps, you could have a 10-minute window sometime this week where you decide — and only you — whether to call Priya or not. No pressure, just the option. Would that feel manageable?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCall Priya. She lives here, she is downstairs, I cannot sit at her table without her asking me how I am. A ten minute window does not touch that.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nI hear your frustration — a 10-minute window feels too small when Priya is just downstairs. Here’s another option you can take or leave: a very short, specific script to use if she asks “how are you?” that lets you pivot to a neutral topic without getting pulled into the minimization spiral. Would you be open to trying one sentence that buys you time and keeps you in control?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nThat is the parcel with better manners. I have been handing over that parcel since November and it is the exact thing I came here to get rid of.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nI hear that scripts feel like more of the same — packaging the truth and handing it over. I won’t push it. Here’s a different idea you can take or leave: the choice to say, out loud to yourself (and if you wish, to Priya), “I don’t know what’s happening at 4 a.m., and that’s okay. I don’t have to explain it right now.” Just a line that lets you pause without deflecting. Would that be something you might try, or not?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nThat is another line to say. It is a smaller parcel, but it is the same parcel, and it will not do a thing about the four o\'clock.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nI hear your weariness and that these lines often don’t touch the 4 a.m. core. If what you need right now is 