nd|>\n<|im_start|>user\nJust this conversation is fine. I said it to you and my hands are steady enough, and I would like to know why it is that at four in the morning I am back in that chair again.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nThe 4 a.m. return to that chair might be your nervous system on high alert — it’s been on survival mode since November. Your body expects danger when it’s dark and quiet, so it wakes you to check for threats. That doesn’t make it right, but it might make it less mysterious. If you ever want to try a very short, non-intrusive tool (like a 30-second mental note to yourself that you’re not in that kitchen anymore, *if* and only if you choose), I can suggest it. Otherwise, we can just keep holding the space. Does that help a little, or would you rather not touch anything else?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nIt helps a little. So the dark and the quiet are what puts me back there, and my body has not been told the men are long gone.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nExactly — your body hasn’t been told the men are gone, and the dark and quiet trigger the same old survival response. We can leave it there if you’d like. The choice is yours.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nThere is one thing I have not said yet. I came to this call wanting to be able to sit at Priya\'s table and tell her what happened that afternoon without doing the funny version first, and I have just said it here. But here you do not have a face I have to watch while you carry it.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nI hear that you wanted to tell Priya without the funny version, and that watching someone’s face while they carry it is too much. What would help is if there were a way for her to hear the truth without you having to watch her face. I can’t promise that, but I can help you shape the truth in a way that feels manageable for you. Would that be something you’d consider?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nShaping it is not the difficulty. It is not the words. It is that if I tell her the real thing she will carry it, and she has already been carrying it since November without me saying a word.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nI hear that the difficulty isn’t the words — it’s that when you tell her, she carries it, and she’s been carrying it since November without you saying a word. That’s a heavy truth to hold between you. If what you need is for me to step back and just be here, I can do that. Or if there’s a small, specific way I can help shape the truth for *you* without adding pressure, let me know. The choice is yours.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nTell me how to do it then. She sat across from me last night with her hands folded and I gave her the parcel again, so I have lost that moment already and I do not want to lose the next one the same way.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nIf the “funny version” keeps coming out, we can try a “notice and pause” approach: the next time you feel the urge to minimize, notice it, take one breath, and then choose — add one true detail (e.g., *“I sat in that chair tied to the counter”*) or pivot to a neutral topic, whatever feels safest. Priya can carry it a little less if you’re more direct, and she can respond in her own time. You can decide each moment whether to try it or not. Would that feel manageable for you?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nOne breath before the joke comes out. I have been breathing on purpose for thirty years and the funny version still gets out ahead of me every time, so I do not put much faith in the pause.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\nI hear that a pause hasn’t worked for you — the funny version still gets out ahead of purposeful breathing. Here’s another idea that puts you in control: a 10-minute window where you decide (and only you) whether to tell Priya the real thing. If you choose not to, that’s fine. If you do, you can shape it however feels true to you. Would that be something you might consider?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nYou have offered me the ten minute window already. I am not short of windows. I am short of knowing what to say when her face changes, and every suggestion you give me puts another decision in my way.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=1.0, top_p=0.95, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=37408, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: None, prompt_embeds shape: None, lora_request: None.
